===============================================================================
P1-17 · Backup via the maintenance utility — FAILURE PATHS
Captured 2026-08-22 on LAPTOP-3HH6OHHE.

Spec section 15, "Failure handling": record error details, display an
operational warning, and require follow-up before acceptance.

WHY THIS FILE IS THE IMPORTANT ONE. A backup system that fails silently is
worse than having none, because it manufactures confidence. The dangerous
behaviour is not an error — it is a run that exits zero, writes a zero-byte
file, and lets a scheduled task report success every night until the day
someone actually needs the backup. Both failure modes below were produced
deliberately and their handling is what this card is really about.
===============================================================================

FAILURE MODE 1 — WRONG CREDENTIALS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The hardest case to handle, because the credential that is wrong for the dump
is the same credential that would be used to RECORD the failure. There is no
database row to write; a database-only record would be no record at all.

$ ... backup --config <config with deliberately wrong password> \
             --directory <scratch>\badrun

WARNING: could not read backup settings from SystemSettings (Access denied
for user 'merch_backup'@'localhost' (using password: YES)). Falling back to
built-in defaults and attempting the backup anyway.

Backup Failed (correlation 01dffc6f-de96-471b-8757-f718860f12c5)
  retention   7 (pruned 0)
  logged      LOCAL FILE ONLY - database unreachable
  ** mysqldump failed (exit code 2). mysqldump.exe: Got error: 1045:
     "Access denied for user 'merch_backup'@'localhost' (using password: YES)"
     when trying to connect

EXIT CODE: 1

Four things to check in that output, each a separate requirement:

  1. EXIT CODE IS NON-ZERO. Task Scheduler shows the job as failed. This is
     the difference between a monitored backup and a decorative one.

  2. THE REAL ERROR IS CARRIED THROUGH VERBATIM — MariaDB error 1045, named
     account, "using password: YES". Not a generic "backup failed". The
     operator can act on this without reproducing it.

  3. NO DUMP FILE IS LEFT BEHIND. Directory listing after the failed run:

        backup-failures.log      334 bytes
        (no .sql file)

     mysqldump creates its --result-file before it authenticates, so a failed
     run leaves an empty file behind. BackupCommand deletes it. This matters
     more than it looks: a zero-byte .sql sitting in the backup directory
     looks like a backup to anyone glancing at the folder, and retention would
     have counted it as one — so a week of failures could have pruned away the
     last good dump while looking healthy.

  4. THE FAILURE IS RECORDED ANYWAY, in the local log, because the database
     was unreachable:

2026-08-22 04:08:06Z [Failed] correlation=01dffc6f-de96-471b-8757-f718860f12c5
detail=mysqldump failed (exit code 2). mysqldump.exe: Got error: 1045:
"Access denied for user 'merch_backup'@'localhost' (using password: YES)" when
trying to connect logWriteError=Access denied for user
'merch_backup'@'localhost' (using password: YES)

     The correlation ID ties the log line to the console output. Both the
     backup failure AND the reason the database record was impossible are
     recorded — the second one is the part that would otherwise vanish.

     No credential appears anywhere in the log line. The defaults-extra-file
     that holds the password is never echoed into the detail text.


FAILURE MODE 2 — OFF-HOST VOLUME NOT ATTACHED
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The USB stick will not be plugged in some nights. This is the common case,
not the exotic one, and it is neither a success nor a failure: the dump is
good and worth keeping, but the off-host copy that protects against losing
the host did not happen.

Proven by integration test Backup_WhenOffHostVolumeAbsent_ReportsPartialNotSucceeded,
and visible in BackupLogs row 2 from the test run:

         Id: 2
     Result: Partial
  SizeBytes: 243446
OffHostPath: NULL

Behaviour:

  - Result is "Partial", never "Succeeded". Reporting success here would hide
    a degraded backup for as long as nobody checked.
  - Result is not "Failed" either. Failing would throw away a perfectly good
    dump and train the operator to ignore the alert.
  - The local dump is KEPT.
  - OffHostPath is NULL rather than a path that does not exist.
  - EXIT CODE IS 1, so the scheduled task still shows red. A partial backup
    that reports success is how a system ends up with months of dumps that
    only ever existed on the machine that died.
  - The warning names the cause: "no ready volume labelled 'MERCHBACKUP' is
    attached. The local dump is the only copy."


FAILURE MODE 3 — DUMP SUCCEEDS BUT CANNOT BE RECORDED
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Handled in code and worth stating even though it is not separately staged: if
the dump works but the BackupLogs insert fails, the outcome is downgraded from
Succeeded to Partial. A dump nothing can prove happened is not a clean
success — nothing downstream can report on it, and the next restore has no
record of what it is restoring.


FAILURE MODE 4 — TRUNCATED DUMP
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Not staged; guarded in production code rather than only in tests. Exit code 0
and a non-empty file together still do not prove the dump ran to the end — a
disk that fills, a killed process, or a connection dropped mid-table all leave
a large, plausible-looking, useless file. mysqldump writes
"-- Dump completed on <timestamp>" as its final line and only reaches it after
every table is out, so BackupCommand.IsUsableDump checks for that marker by
reading the last 512 bytes.

This check was added because a test assertion was wrong: it asserted the
header said "MySQL dump" when this build writes "MariaDB dump 10.19, Distrib
10.4.32-MariaDB". Fixing the assertion prompted the question of what would
actually prove a dump was whole, and the completion marker is the answer.


WHAT REMAINS OWED
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Spec section 15 requires failures to "require follow-up before acceptance".
The mechanism here is the non-zero exit code surfacing in Task Scheduler plus
the recorded row or log line. There is no alerting beyond that — no email, no
dashboard — and for a system with no operations team that is the honest
scope. An operator who never opens Task Scheduler will not learn of a failure.
